0, \"issueLinks\": 0, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 2, \"description\": \"In client commands there are two problems involving pystachio bindings:(1) Binding parameters sometimes don\'t work correctly;(2) If a binding never gets resolved an error isn\'t generated.\", \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 3, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',126,'AURORA-649','39','{\"key\": \"AURORA-649\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 0, \"sprint\": 39, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"Maxim Khutornenko\", \"summary\": \"Implement scheduler API methods linking updater logic and storage\", \"assignee\": \"Maxim Khutornenko\", \"priority\": 3, \"reporter\": \"Maxim Khutornenko\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 0, \"issueType\": \"Task\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 0, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 1, \"description\": null, \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 7, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',127,'AURORA-772','39','{\"key\": \"AURORA-772\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 0, \"sprint\": 39, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"David McLaughlin\", \"summary\": \"Add username to the update events\", \"assignee\": \"David McLaughlin\", \"priority\": 3, \"reporter\": \"David McLaughlin\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 0, \"issueType\": \"Task\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 0, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 1, \"description\": \"Depending on your auth scheme different users can perform operations on a single update. E.g. one user can start the update but another user pause and resume it. To be able to reveal this information when displaying updates we should attach the user to each JobUpdateEvent instance. \", \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 1, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',128,'AURORA-741','39','{\"key\": \"AURORA-741\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 1, \"sprint\": 39, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"Bill Farner\", \"summary\": \"DbJobUpdateStore can\'t fetch updates that have no initial state\", \"assignee\": \"Maxim Khutornenko\", \"priority\": 1, \"reporter\": \"Bill Farner\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 1, \"issueType\": \"Story\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 1, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 2, \"description\": \"If an IJobUpdate has an empty set for {{instructions.initialState}} it can be saved but not retrieved.Here\'s an example stack trace where this was encountered{noformat}java.lang.IllegalStateException: Optional.get() cannot be called on an absent valueat com.google.common.base.Absent.get(Absent.java:47)at org.apache.aurora.scheduler.updater.JobUpdateControllerImpl.changeJobUpdateStatus(JobUpdateControllerImpl.java:388)at org.apache.aurora.scheduler.updater.JobUpdateControllerImpl.recordAndChangeJobUpdateStatus(JobUpdateControllerImpl.java:330)at org.apache.aurora.scheduler.updater.JobUpdateControllerImpl.access$200(JobUpdateControllerImpl.java:93)at org.apache.aurora.scheduler.updater.JobUpdateControllerImpl$1.execute(JobUpdateControllerImpl.java:151)at org.apache.aurora.scheduler.storage.Storage$MutateWork$NoResult.apply(Storage.java:131)at org.apache.aurora.scheduler.storage.Storage$MutateWork$NoResult.apply(Storage.java:127)at org.apache.aurora.scheduler.storage.mem.MemStorage.doWork(MemStorage.java:175)at org.apache.aurora.scheduler.storage.mem.MemStorage.access$400(MemStorage.java:59)at org.apache.aurora.scheduler.storage.mem.MemStorage$4.apply(MemStorage.java:200)at org.apache.aurora.scheduler.storage.mem.MemStorage$4.apply(MemStorage.java:197)at org.apache.aurora.scheduler.storage.db.DbStorage.write(DbStorage.java:150)at org.mybatis.guice.transactional.TransactionalMethodInterceptor.invoke(TransactionalMethodInterceptor.java:101)at org.apache.aurora.scheduler.storage.mem.MemStorage.write(MemStorage.java:197)at org.apache.aurora.scheduler.updater.JobUpdateControllerImpl.start(JobUpdateControllerImpl.java:131){noformat}\", \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 1, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',129,'AURORA-187','39','{\"key\": \"AURORA-187\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 0, \"sprint\": 39, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"Brian Wickman\", \"summary\": \"scheduler failover should never abort an update\", \"assignee\": \"Maxim Khutornenko\", \"priority\": 2, \"reporter\": \"Brian Wickman\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 0, \"issueType\": \"Bug\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 0, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 2, \"description\": \"User reported they had an aborted update{noformat} INFO] Response from scheduler: ERROR (message: Aborting update without rollback! Fatal error: Storage is not READY){noformat}This seems like a regression.  Users should never get aborted updates due to scheduler failovers.\", \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 2, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',130,'AURORA-733','39','{\"key\": \"AURORA-733\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 0, \"sprint\": 39, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"Bill Farner\", \"summary\": \"MedianAlgorithm results should be windowed in time\", \"assignee\": \"Maxim Khutornenko\", \"priority\": 3, \"reporter\": \"Bill Farner\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 0, \"issueType\": \"Story\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 0, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 2, \"description\": \"{{MedianAlgorithm}} computes the median of numeric values extracted from {{IScheduledTask}} s.  Unlike other {{SlaAlgorithm}} implementations it ignores the {{timeFrame}} argument and computes the median for all tasks provided.  This results in a graph that is historically-weighted and less responsive to load.  Consider using the {{timeFrame}} to filter tasks before computing the mdeian.\", \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 1, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',131,'AURORA-686','39','{\"key\": \"AURORA-686\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 0, \"sprint\": 39, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"Maxim Khutornenko\", \"summary\": \"Job updates may fail due to exceeding role quota\", \"assignee\": \"Maxim Khutornenko\", \"priority\": 3, \"reporter\": \"Maxim Khutornenko\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 0, \"issueType\": \"Story\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 1, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 1, \"description\": \"Current way of checking job quota during in-flight updates (i.e. within addInstance transaction) may lead to failed updates and inferior user experience. Since we are tracking quota at the role level but the update lock applied at the job level there is always a possibility to exceed the allowed quota for long running updates. This is especially a problem with the server side-driven process where a resumed update will restart in a potentially quite different quota environment (i.e. due to other jobs created while the update was paused). Possible solutions:- per job quota tracking - requires significant refactoring;- hierarchical locking (e.g. add role lock in addition to job lock) - limits update concurrency per role;- front-loaded consumption (e.g. add additional job consumption during startJobUpdate and re-evaluate on update completion/termination) - will require persisting front-loaded value within job update schema but may be the way to go given current quota implementation.  \", \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 1, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',132,'AURORA-412','39','{\"key\": \"AURORA-412\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 0, \"sprint\": 39, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"Brian Wickman\", \"summary\": \"Upgrade psutil once post-2.1.1 release is cut\", \"assignee\": \"Brian Wickman\", \"priority\": 3, \"reporter\": \"Brian Wickman\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 0, \"issueType\": \"Task\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 0, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 2, \"description\": \"We\'ve tickled https://code.google.com/p/psutil/issues/detail?id=503 in production a few times.  This is now patched on psutil master but a release has not yet been cut.  Once that happens we should upgrade to a newer release.\", \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 3, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',133,'AURORA-737','39','{\"key\": \"AURORA-737\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 0, \"sprint\": 39, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"Mark Chu-Carroll\", \"summary\": \"Improve clientv2 usage helplines.\", \"assignee\": \"Mark Chu-Carroll\", \"priority\": 3, \"reporter\": \"Mark Chu-Carroll\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 0, \"issueType\": \"Task\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 0, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 1, \"description\": \"This is created in response to user complaints.The \\\"usage\\\" line in aurora clientv2 help is automatically generated from command options. When an option doesn\'t explicitly declare a string to be used as the displayname of an option the system defaults to using the type of the parameter. For some parameters this is perfectly fine. For example\\\"--watch-secs=int\\\" is clear about what it expects.In other places though it\'s very unclear. For example for creating a job the user sees:{noformat}  create [--bind=pystachio-binding] [--read-json] [--wait-until=(\'PENDING\' \'RUNNING\' \'FINISHED\')] [--open-browser] CLUSTER/ROLE/ENV/NAME str{noformat}What is the \\\"str\\\" supposed to mean? In all of  the places where the typename is displayed and its meaning is unclear the code should explicitly specify a display name/format for the parameter.\", \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 1, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',134,'AURORA-718','39','{\"key\": \"AURORA-718\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 1, \"sprint\": 39, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"Bill Farner\", \"summary\": \"JobUpdateConfiguration should reflect work required for a job update\", \"assignee\": \"Bill Farner\", \"priority\": 3, \"reporter\": \"Bill Farner\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 1, \"issueType\": \"Story\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 3, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 1, \"description\": \"JobUpdateConfiguration contains three fields to reflect the \'before\' and \'after\' state of a job: instanceCount newTaskConfig oldTaskConfigs.  These could be leveraged to do the same but also show the \'actual work\' required for the update.  For example in an update that is scoped to specific instances or where not all instances need to be modified (no-ops) these fields should indicate which instances the scheduler needs to modify.This ticket tracks a change to how oldTaskConfigs is computed.  AURORA-717 tracks conversion of the other two fields.\", \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 1, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',135,'AURORA-755','39','{\"key\": \"AURORA-755\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 0, \"sprint\": 39, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"David McLaughlin\", \"summary\": \"Issue with partial deploys in the update instance summary\", \"assignee\": \"David McLaughlin\", \"priority\": 3, \"reporter\": \"David McLaughlin\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 0, \"issueType\": \"Story\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 0, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 1, \"description\": \"The new update UI doesn\'t seem to handle the use case of increasing instance count of a job with the same config as the old one. \", \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 1, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',136,'AURORA-745','39','{\"key\": \"AURORA-745\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 0, \"sprint\": 39, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"David McLaughlin\", \"summary\": \"Show in-flight and recently completed updates in scheduler UI\", \"assignee\": \"David McLaughlin\", \"priority\": 3, \"reporter\": \"David McLaughlin\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 0, \"issueType\": \"Story\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 0, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 1, \"description\": \"We should add a page just to summarise all currently in progress updates as well as showing recently completed updates. \", \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 1, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',137,'AURORA-764','39','{\"key\": \"AURORA-764\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 0, \"sprint\": 39, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"Bill Farner\", \"summary\": \"Stop sending deleted task events ignore incoming deleted task events.\", \"assignee\": \"Bill Farner\", \"priority\": 3, \"reporter\": \"Bill Farner\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 0, \"issueType\": \"Story\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 0, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 1, \"description\": null, \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 1, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',138,'AURORA-84','39','{\"key\": \"AURORA-84\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 0, \"sprint\": 39, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"Bill Farner\", \"summary\": \"Deprecate the Identity struct\", \"assignee\": \"Maxim Khutornenko\", \"priority\": 3, \"reporter\": \"Bill Farner\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 0, \"issueType\": \"Task\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 1, \"storyPoint\": null, \"watchcount\": 6, \"description\": \"The Identity field is nested in several structs and the {{role}} field it contains is ~always redundant to a field in {{JobKey}}. Consider removing Identity and using (or adding) a non-user-bearing field like JobKey.An open question is whether whether {{Identity.user}} should be retained.  Most likely yes.\", \"fixVersions\": 1, \"commentCount\": 29, \"dependedOnBy\": 0, \"affectedVersions\": 0}'),('Aurora',139,'AURORA-798','42','{\"key\": \"AURORA-798\", \"votes\": 0, \"blocks\": 0, \"sprint\": 42, \"status\": \"Resolved\", \"creator\": \"Kevin Sweeney\", \"summary\": \"investigate flaky test: LockManagerImplTest/testNoDeadlock\", \"assignee\": \"Maxim Khutornenko\", \"priority\": 3, \"reporter\": \"Kevin Sweeney\", \"subtasks\": 0, \"blockedBy\": 0, \"issueType\": \"Bug\", \"dependedOn\": 0, \"issueLinks\": 0, \"s